#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198011 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "3b27595a-5d6d-5ea3-92d2-6de7895c19fa" :question/math-academy-id "e-11153" :question/problem "![](images/93/93c82014d308d19ebfc10e7046ac1e8b6cf78017b6bc10e0671f43d22aac996a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 2, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the right side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u = 2$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u = 2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8b/8b14a175f7931dda3e3b6afd97c5219c390a0674f147289a257e0e655dcc11f1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "b9d1a634-f1ef-5130-83ca-f3a555027699" :question/math-academy-id "e-12332" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a869685bd7163515672154b20c2a41a50b5df320bae30e650d7a6d0b7d44ee1.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (x(u,v) , \\, y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y= u \\sin v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $\\Delta$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Sketch the region $\\Delta.$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $\\mathbf{T},$ we have\n\n$$\nx = u \\cos v, \\qquad y = u \\sin v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $\\Delta$ of $D$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  \\sqrt 2 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{12}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\sqrt 2$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ and $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c8/c83bd9d913d4959ad275ab4420b647cd030fff45438f59d026e3658bd146a1ee.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198019 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "4eaf3262-b6e3-57bc-9662-42ab52626ee9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12340" :question/problem "![](images/65/6515550b9a280f423cdc2a2e89fd83014733c0783fdd4b2b2966e6a4dd37492a.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the square region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\bigg\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4}  \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\bigg\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $\\Delta$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. It lies between the first and second quadrants between the polar rays $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ and $\\theta=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/79/79869cf64ec827934397588e25d06cba0c9d12c35ecbea5cdd6aaf6cfcf12e24.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "f4539253-b127-5d96-a331-3b357dc08a9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12363" :question/problem "![](images/86/86a240b5c7c1fc945e12da274be7e9cac14c1b5a9add004d52d72390afb39aff.png)\n\n     \n\nConsider the rectangular region $\\Delta$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (u,v) \\: :  \\:  1 \\le u \\le 3, \\:\\: \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} \\leq v \\leq \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3} \\right\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $\\mathbf T,$ given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:(u,v) \\to (u \\cos v, \\, u \\sin v).\n$$\n\nDetermine the image of the top side of $\\Delta$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $\\Delta$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ and $1 \\leq u \\leq 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v = \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,1\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/81/81650cc0dc43537ec916f2c68af66ed5581c861812f276dda2b23baccc2fb491.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27058026-eaa9-50cf-a7ff-85a98dcd27a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/17/17ec72b3de492831d262ccf4d969134caeb6e68d43d733bc93c58f23ab5514a8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275832} {:answer/id #uuid "477b0483-514f-56d1-9f3e-80f37988204f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c9/c91abe36d6252dfbdb0e01a9698a7215a7b1f5b1b62d8559f8579790f4742642.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276107} {:answer/id #uuid "92fdeb38-ffe1-59a8-8eb4-37396968c059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d6/d6f844bfa587d36ffe72ed5742e2e00ca26a47da9dc4ca14aeed194fa583fd71.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276810} {:answer/id #uuid "acaa5388-a14e-5da1-aa4a-d672a30c06fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277030} {:answer/id #uuid "bd713ed7-9bf0-5a4c-be1b-525ad169b374" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/69/6947eab8954d579db57427e62bbf501b1a47a1cf3d554544e5776ed3114573b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "acaa5388-a14e-5da1-aa4a-d672a30c06fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277030} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ea82fdd-ea50-5bff-a3e3-6a33754c4288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e90b093e-e3d7-5a59-aa98-97c5665b0473" :question/math-academy-id "q-160414" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f899ac64f4d16cc14255603642e9038ec0b76f6b3ec27da53d3692b25431444.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=\\sqrt{2},$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/61/616f6f5e952fa5634497b86ced1893c938cd76051ae0e0e0200bb323bff33238.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d2076bad-e450-5d12-88e1-ecdc82f3c6b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9e/9eff6be311d55584343d654622aa4851ecd2a55f29d2828e6b2fc5053a92d6aa.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194100} {:answer/id #uuid "f54bab40-b8b3-50b2-94f5-570d411ba9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/58/58bb97be2f7cdc125fb1df3eab8d9189dbef1c309da426ff202a08ed8ec9d1d6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194101} {:answer/id #uuid "556f1dc8-7c68-5b36-a1db-428d1435f96f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e653f100000b2c41357f1914c27f41f26a197771a69cdf6c2ec03be48d7c3a24.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194102} {:answer/id #uuid "6598ea6f-24da-5192-b8dd-6126b77dfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b221864b2795f2b158e8c4d1f88da23437f8d043da6c31e0b5c8392ebc1118bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194103} {:answer/id #uuid "7cf7b9f4-d688-596d-a4bb-219387bfe184" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/de/de6e9757abe6322b1678303f398f79643e809b3f26858a4e5a10c22173be568d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6598ea6f-24da-5192-b8dd-6126b77dfbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b2/b221864b2795f2b158e8c4d1f88da23437f8d043da6c31e0b5c8392ebc1118bd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194103} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5e66fcd-0510-5aa7-ab10-61ee829bd568" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2fca64ae-782f-5ae2-bf69-6525c7a1e239" :question/math-academy-id "q-160418" :question/problem "![](images/41/413ff5566eedb8e99c8fb0f3d29b8effd0e146ee4367db93264a5392edd818c1.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=2,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b2/b221864b2795f2b158e8c4d1f88da23437f8d043da6c31e0b5c8392ebc1118bd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1edd58eb-2609-5289-b8db-1ccaf640d414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275756} {:answer/id #uuid "50fcad8d-7b33-5d0f-990b-a5f36613c5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276201} {:answer/id #uuid "b1e453d5-546f-55d4-bc04-ca32ac633bce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277068} {:answer/id #uuid "b3977036-2465-5c28-8434-402f0de670f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277083} {:answer/id #uuid "c14de4ff-288a-5275-8a79-32b77d250968" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b3977036-2465-5c28-8434-402f0de670f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277083} :answer-field/id #uuid "af64bec7-80b6-5600-b765-33bb20ecc3e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9eb2357b-f9d2-552f-8ddb-db8ea02b5422" :question/math-academy-id "q-160481" :question/problem "![](images/b8/b8874b14fa80881e69cb1daee180d2e342a27c5902e66fc75d43622278176b09.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcfc165548d8174ec0468dce53317fd0c8daa14565852c5e44d6764161723bed.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true, while statement II is false. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\n- When $u=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3f97954b-fab9-555e-a63d-0b7ff6619a7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82557cc5a33963a7ec6d7910da8dc1409fd2cdc1245b00be009f7a237bee6c3a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389079} {:answer/id #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389080} {:answer/id #uuid "35760d42-f2ba-5032-8da1-1ca1d76889e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/12/128955fddc503b24eb73c5a321a803a4318f16e83e88f1d28be0a0e621f926c9.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389081} {:answer/id #uuid "fde379d9-5306-507a-a69f-f50ff3931c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1b/1b2026b5c41f64f72aa9d1c849f9357f4192860545e9a41241c2a0f87d33db6a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389082} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a07a20-8567-5538-9f06-4d6de140aa40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/70/709f9c571b81383cb436570ea4c63575a2fb7e83b1c2e377a6347f54c8ceea35.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f425ff2-e50f-5b19-ab10-931b2a0a25ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389080} :answer-field/id #uuid "79e6b72a-de31-55a4-8613-ca44de670c50" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "31d74e75-fb66-5eb8-9774-1cd2896eeaa8" :question/math-academy-id "q-160909" :question/problem "![](images/95/951c3d3bca4116deebccbbabb5d7405bb07669cacb83c1e450204bb40c247d5c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 2.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{2\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=2$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=2$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=2,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/20/20f36ce6a4a889d6906f11dff0c4f0ffb5f6e55a857c29f5e4491f0f098b658f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/97ada65e0942c57cfaae530a099d183ade03377955766c21707192cf534c57a7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423457} {:answer/id #uuid "28df0d1a-86ac-5691-b099-21f856eccfac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f2/f22991a62043a8fbd1e27ea16a103a830fa5e3874517e9c84d6b11f9dac0702e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423459} {:answer/id #uuid "2d848126-3113-59a0-965e-db5f69875346" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/62/62156bad25518bbc8e9c324a2906ba804e6dc38edd37a752bd3092f2313ebea0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423461} {:answer/id #uuid "7ff779d8-2902-5dbe-9b08-74d03fa4ab9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/dc/dc641778741dd5fbe43c8edad8a34f033e898eb279a7fa680b44f75aaebaabda.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423469} {:answer/id #uuid "94357291-cfe9-5d56-86c6-275da88ed2a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/56/5663dfd0afc5b44954f73064b69aedcd33b983755fc7a425f4b963b3bad665eb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "160c880d-89ca-54d9-91a4-23ab7095bd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/97/97ada65e0942c57cfaae530a099d183ade03377955766c21707192cf534c57a7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423457} :answer-field/id #uuid "d62a4acb-ad20-5c4f-b6b4-f3aea150ce3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "22e295c4-59c2-57da-8c38-5051b16dc6ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-160923" :question/problem "![](images/f3/f370177dc8119044b74fd4923c3d4318d5b9e1b4de89b63967284ee1cd7794da.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/03dae0fbacc0339613580414c2217652b9ff6303788c7ca265deb3b1f755f4cd.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423502 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f7366f0-ea26-5a4d-ad99-5c479c1363cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8890d6bf89507aff0334f26d0b906f260b3d5e00db974cdef59faf17d5068b1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423458} {:answer/id #uuid "8f0aeef6-20b2-5bbf-b82d-cf19738fc0b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4c/4cbda09d23f88ee9b7c87632180f10fbe0c678b3c8ac6b8b183bbc82fd43c774.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423472} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6dca9e-c430-5e66-b053-f24ceb1406ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/db/db98f6bc045f3f3a6bd7d22e061861b2d00182af6968b9562b59f22248d94f6b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423473} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d258ef-d6de-5ab7-9099-98537e9ff4a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/90/909d48b33e74f7bdc0bd18d639325a88f7313126cadee7f367183541897e231e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423479} {:answer/id #uuid "caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423484}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "caa435c1-c17e-509a-bc0c-1d20be69dbc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423484} :answer-field/id #uuid "a914c4df-a2e3-591c-b430-880a8ee5c996" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423492}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ff752515-0aa8-5668-9f6f-2eed5f67dc87" :question/math-academy-id "q-160924" :question/problem "![](images/19/19f21fa7abb989e8c2bf35552675f498969e30e1f42012fd615c00686d20cb01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ and $1\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{6\\pi }{7})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{6\\pi }{7}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $1$ and $3$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\le v\\le \\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=1$ and $r=3,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{5}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/50/50462754e89259447fe464f3a9c8ca7f165260d4b7adfc25585912291091838b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198377 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4068f861-26ff-582b-93c5-15f2fd44565d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Rectangle Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1313dcb9-789f-52c3-a390-bf401c7faaaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2eb8b5f7ee3fe1b85960f1d6c87a2aabf58aae6f97394b2c7145cc98b39708dc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275652} {:answer/id #uuid "814d65b2-5d38-5263-8c18-7ac197ebae2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2e/2e8246c7764ee6a059da534fa078f3d3c7caebfbc55554be2da63002da2007bc.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276643} {:answer/id #uuid "d9773844-06e5-5a9a-83d3-ef24b5bca0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a0/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277386} {:answer/id #uuid "dd1cfb4e-7f09-57b2-a8aa-c2a14ef59c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1c/1c9d7de1e895affd9f75deda09fe4b3243ea39a04b47198309e0b52c6197b2f8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277412} {:answer/id #uuid "e46013f2-875c-5239-9c25-469122032ba7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/37/37bccae9033e5eac9a48f8d3d570d69d1a382f26646617665c75f32a9ac7ab4e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277473}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d9773844-06e5-5a9a-83d3-ef24b5bca0fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a0/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277386} :answer-field/id #uuid "31ef5b68-ee3e-596e-99c2-fb71f74c0fee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1bfb95ad-c905-51fe-8cac-7a80399c1f9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-160925" :question/problem "![](images/c8/c86259b973101d17d732f4ac8c29a1b61de9fadfc67424a2ab6d28a2ceaa9a01.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nFind the image of $Δ$ under the action of $T.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we transform each side of $Δ$ separately:\n\n- Consider the image of the bottom side, where we have $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{6})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the top side, where we have $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{5})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis line segment makes an angle of $\\frac{9\\pi }{5}$ with the positive $x$-axis in the $xy$-plane. Moreover, this segment lies on a line that passes through the origin, and its endpoints lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $4$ centered at $O.$\n\n- Consider the image of the left side, where we have $u=\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{2}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\n- Consider the image of the right side, where we have $u=4$ and $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=4$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y)=(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an arc of the circle of radius $4$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. The arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the image $D$ of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is the region that is enclosed between \n\n- two circles with polar equations $r=\\sqrt{2}$ and $r=4,$ and\n\n- two rays with polar equations $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{9\\pi }{5},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0beb4e2dfd69e902b927915771985d9e8180ec7910993c3485cc85002956d1e.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278346 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15595742-55cf-5dc2-acd6-fdd7e56cd42b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275678} {:answer/id #uuid "450cc431-4419-55db-ad09-3474170a9089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9f/9f3fd1164ab4346961dab3ab258cf06ccd7aea62644375feeb2b24690c3ca718.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276085} {:answer/id #uuid "47eaa59e-842a-50cb-8208-dbfc58c0018c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b09b73c500e5c08dd443f1a53f8fbc1a644d18bcd90aa3e96bc1a02e77db75b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276114} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc1018c-0ee4-54f6-a087-5e477a84ed37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/49/49ea0e312d1572a3e598ae1962fac6fd10e61835b40c73310792857eb0dc2380.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276764} {:answer/id #uuid "c883ab2c-6d9e-50cd-8753-3104ae879c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c883ab2c-6d9e-50cd-8753-3104ae879c36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/32/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277260} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee01c459-7210-576f-9970-8c94b5818c33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278144}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb782379-9f8a-586a-90e0-b8ba4fd007cb" :question/math-academy-id "q-160942" :question/problem "![](images/04/0412da40e0e04e8da5ec92db1ad665251209a718333ec008ceeebbe308c71855.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=0$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=0$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(0\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,0\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(0,\\,0),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the point $(0,0)$ in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/32aa42ca7dc665fb0a486f9a4f984916857d3964934c7a74c50723f1018fd833.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be52c672-2de5-56f0-9576-cf2f1cb2c3c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/df/df47e830c98a8f5e3ddfdf909ec23163735b21486eb25d784bb6b88ea95e4f60.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389100} {:answer/id #uuid "282b979d-0b3b-5d2d-b38c-80725bf830b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/77/772ffafe5ebbe9aad24464f1a4ee7937c2a14895f62d6a73e0f1422a0bc899ee.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389101} {:answer/id #uuid "4892dc8f-6ac8-516f-82fe-ebb19d4650d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389102} {:answer/id #uuid "3206686d-f296-5283-8260-05891a5656e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389103} {:answer/id #uuid "fbbd466a-6afa-5c79-99ef-7a3166b990c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e8/e8ed422a10846fc3f44fb894d1051f184dde24b5a38181f25366ba209c5986c5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4892dc8f-6ac8-516f-82fe-ebb19d4650d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389102} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e79051-6fca-5a52-820a-2af4080980ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9c0c66a8-0dcc-5684-892c-2aa4d891f439" :question/math-academy-id "q-160944" :question/problem "![](images/6e/6e0e9b7d2cbff011a5ff209f335fb3d5e86af02a58c4ea5c8a8d46bd384e6a0c.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=\\sqrt{3}$ and $0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=\\sqrt{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =0$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/20/2051bbb1a83dca354f57ff21a1e7208d9acbd9b4122ba8e7406933b449199b52.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278347 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ebaa46-ae1a-5015-968e-8dacf1cf46a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b611546ee3a8ae2714de1cc97dec62b9c750a39f858864b63d70be0d26a65b68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275719} {:answer/id #uuid "1e0b9507-1575-546a-998c-cf251eebe223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2b/2be15ae2d8bf7a309065ac33c97f5937d3fc8c12c5ca6ddc0c54ab44f555a5d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275753} {:answer/id #uuid "31dac6f4-d28a-5e3d-b4a7-01d21c309dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/549d8c8e2e6cf9eb785c0c0f80ddfe95bfdb51429a6797368b97908241ef713c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275918} {:answer/id #uuid "32a6fab1-7882-5604-ad53-77b038189abc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275926} {:answer/id #uuid "4212671c-62c1-5198-8f3d-7e247861f0b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9a/9a40e3dbc6eeecaf9b33830b762e75f54d7ffea5c1dd9ab9ae6e2bd9cca721e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276055}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31dac6f4-d28a-5e3d-b4a7-01d21c309dbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/54/549d8c8e2e6cf9eb785c0c0f80ddfe95bfdb51429a6797368b97908241ef713c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275918} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a69f55d-21f4-537d-a3d4-1cdb1565e837" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f157d340-55fe-5182-98c8-272f8763a3d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-160945" :question/problem "![](images/29/2932639855f3031da6adcca7eaee38b992a145809f119902e93958fee47ff2a6.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=1$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=1$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(1\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,1\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $1$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the first quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b8/b829ff7e7a51ed9f8089b0b8fc22f727ccf5dc7b2e937ec90f6a1affb8d29064.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423499 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06d4b6b0-a358-5d2b-800f-323c9e6650be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b4/b49ec400c11b5a840ef6616dea072b7dd983c75ffbaf7071b0abc6e946d437e0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423454} {:answer/id #uuid "0e9607ce-f279-5606-a106-4f06825e56cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/29/29f3ad97013b5bb588110ac919f3900801495b7f6a38ec99cae83388cc5e26d1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423455} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfa80f7-dab2-569b-88e4-c82c59bd950f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/61/610bad214eeb3c16902775557b3f6badae765235fd6a8ec041f94974b114cf6c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423460} {:answer/id #uuid "a595837c-4b77-52a2-910f-992287910304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ea/eaae0415448f800d52eeb1fc13a927b41e9e9d180d1006ce4012d5d72a5864b0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423477} {:answer/id #uuid "ded9cc47-a1a2-5ddf-be65-d06a1abaac16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b82293b9940cff262ec5601f8652df8aa739fb7e3d1cf66a323cff4d1c46ff0f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423487}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ded9cc47-a1a2-5ddf-be65-d06a1abaac16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b8/b82293b9940cff262ec5601f8652df8aa739fb7e3d1cf66a323cff4d1c46ff0f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423487} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bc5b6cd-4460-56f9-ace4-a472d1cd6181" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b54a0f96-7cef-5900-9667-b793d1ee0611" :question/math-academy-id "q-160947" :question/problem "![](images/16/1686ee5f02ab2215b700216f8dd0844d4763d0f3bde9837cb7913d2790a9c310.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\frac{3}{2},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\frac{3}{2},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/b8/b82293b9940cff262ec5601f8652df8aa739fb7e3d1cf66a323cff4d1c46ff0f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "218d67cb-db13-561d-9a92-de210ba958f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/59/59fbcb01193bfc07615f1f321a5417c1ad9c2819a9befc9a2745e73f791f6e48.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275781} {:answer/id #uuid "8e29e031-f812-5ead-b8c3-91f47ae68155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276768} {:answer/id #uuid "8e7dfb81-461e-5a42-bf8f-f9ad4e0483fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276770} {:answer/id #uuid "9c3898ce-115e-5a24-b1b4-ac0eeaba5922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9b/9bfcd7c758af688acfdec1ce287838470539fbb2fea728a54810f08193b5246a.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276877} {:answer/id #uuid "bc1da3cc-7d49-5d69-9c09-3a6c2633b156" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1e/1e08a502271231c6594e3f6924d5d8791c1ff2f4a9988d18d3e8c0cecde3ee92.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e7dfb81-461e-5a42-bf8f-f9ad4e0483fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3b/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276770} :answer-field/id #uuid "1da59042-9d65-53b8-9650-455cc6854e75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277778}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "019ee70c-3708-5fe3-b8b1-31b6dfb8e888" :question/math-academy-id "q-160967" :question/problem "![](images/21/21952fe273fd1a1ecb03727f4aa254d4e0ecdab2feaeb1d8c652bc9923404846.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,3\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the left side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the left side.\n\nFor the left side, we have $u=3$ and $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=3$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,3\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\le v\\le \\frac{13\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $3$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the third quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{12}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{9},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/3b/3b8b1c7b2a7e68aee7a685e946a0b248470dbe4c5d4dcdfec9cd22783529345c.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423500 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198369 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "992d4af7-bdf3-50ef-b524-bc609271541a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Vertical Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f3549c5-ab21-5d1a-90d8-9ee9169a75ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ac/ac62c64d3a078be5432e7cdca17212226d7f11924878678aeef7b1fc18ebed07.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423468} {:answer/id #uuid "82b1b19a-a349-584f-a6da-2df60e51ccbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423470} {:answer/id #uuid "8e06a460-44eb-5e1a-a4e7-d7c2cfd8e89e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423471} {:answer/id #uuid "a10f5005-7b34-598d-9b41-d0cd54665e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/51/51344600fafe3b69a5a055bae6b5691ae72f7af923eef64062f4502a0c6dbc2d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423476} {:answer/id #uuid "b1629f56-2fe8-5dd2-9891-1c223dd671bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/88/882d8af547164ee97fde649bd178a6c57d78176e2f40b0878ee8dcb0cf61aa68.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82b1b19a-a349-584f-a6da-2df60e51ccbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423470} :answer-field/id #uuid "8362e8f8-2931-5481-95c5-afb548403e1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b5a18f4e-2d75-5904-b5e5-ab7ba2838493" :question/math-academy-id "q-160968" :question/problem "![](images/93/93132c9352ed5068001c824741a1c0a7cb8dca82564ad91db706bc1ecfcedbcc.png)\n\nConsider the rectangular region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 6,\\,\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the right side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the right side.\n\nFor the right side, we have $u=6$ and $\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nSubstituting $u=6$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,6\\operatorname{sin}⁡v),\\,\\frac{14\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{11\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an equation of a circular arc of radius $6$ centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane. \nThe arc lies in the fourth quadrant between the polar rays $\\theta =\\frac{14\\pi }{9}$ and $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/b6/b6052c625de5a1d392975afae8c1053b2b430859ba6fbc3bc8c2c3de01d40318.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423456} {:answer/id #uuid "332853ed-2acb-5257-876f-53cb0dc7f6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423462} {:answer/id #uuid "33fa1164-e443-5847-962d-f5038cdc0390" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/1a/1a52a5fba437ff08657af756644f5d4569d35bd7598b92721423103d8c308ca2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423463} {:answer/id #uuid "493f0a23-3da5-598b-a8a0-9acfa743f9c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/74/74d029bf33b8c0e978006a0fb066843abf4c2dd0fc4c8ea7d125ae53b1626570.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423465} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba7decd-30d3-50fb-b574-0b6278fb2388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/34/34be2d9120d93014b0d176aca946ebaf310ccdd574285c487c26a8fe00778412.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa43f41-195e-5521-a4ae-5695230229e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423456} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb72ef15-e2d9-5b72-84ec-e98850448e17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423493}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3e5df847-265a-5b9c-9720-3136b8ccb366" :question/math-academy-id "q-161024" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a44852c606e67842a58ad1a696253e6e211816f8243beec8b3eea97ef42055a2.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{9}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ and $\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{9})),\\,\\sqrt{2}\\le u\\le 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis and whose endpoints lie on circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $3$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c5ef04b3fa4765bbad1bd7345fe5217feac73c21251b99426c17ed22ec3bbd1.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584874cf-45f7-5bee-ad4c-cff9dd61fafe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/2d/2d95ce1c0b844b49000b05ef44ef0da8b516740804a8e6bd275a1f9ca15b2ed0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276269} {:answer/id #uuid "7d3dfe37-6205-5422-b918-e6d81b4ee49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276619} {:answer/id #uuid "878dfa7e-92cb-5c8c-b42e-c3016b96b8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a63ab6b0cb9e525901e8173ae54afb89530ae96342975731b76e51b2a51d752e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276700} {:answer/id #uuid "a2283404-74f4-597b-8d72-caae8132ba82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The image is the empty set" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276938} {:answer/id #uuid "dba6cfae-b425-5d87-a3c3-aeb9c7548d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/53/539932b6d0bc70809848bf1690df34dc56ebb569dc4877c231bd08e8698c5764.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d3dfe37-6205-5422-b918-e6d81b4ee49d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/48/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276619} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c400c5-8573-5465-a3ff-27207f2d93f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e82f4301-a070-5bc8-9a49-2d57b54ecb56" :question/math-academy-id "q-161028" :question/problem "![](images/aa/aa62dda1abb7bc6bca1b2e6cdf356b52946820912ec6318e5901273a546ee96a.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nWhich of the following could be the image of the bottom side of $Δ$ under the action of $T?$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the bottom side.\n\nFor the bottom side, we have $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $0\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})),\\,0\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment that makes an angle of $\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ with the positive $x$-axis, where one endpoint is the point $O,$ while the other endpoint lies on the circle of radius $4$ centered at $O,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/48/48eb6a022826e8f4e1246201e4ad190fff9bb6afdf7b2146d89010e76478a4bf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423464} {:answer/id #uuid "97210339-8c0b-5905-a2df-f3b57f435e8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423475} {:answer/id #uuid "c03a15a1-3e13-53ce-9c92-762f8bc2417d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423483} {:answer/id #uuid "cc45f2dd-0a53-53f5-b140-3b2a83fa50cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423485} {:answer/id #uuid "d1969766-6b47-59dc-a429-472653bbb6d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423486}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45672c57-a19d-5a8b-ac6b-6ea21293b5d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423464} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7528fa6-219c-52b3-82fe-4826326f572d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423495}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa0b27c3-d4dd-5c0c-8989-66af8729df06" :question/math-academy-id "q-161029" :question/problem "![](images/12/1252844f7a9e2fb2ca0ff1d2e2e1546a460e7d0846b4ae2996b6ce984ee305a1.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{5\\pi }{12}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $0$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ and $\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\le u\\le 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/49/49aa7d6ccd4b14b48ede7486f41ab11ac22b593c06d75795fe7819889b2b2f23.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{12}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is true.\n\n- When $u=\\frac{1}{4},$ we obtain the point \n$(\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\n- When $u=1,$ we obtain the point \n$(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12}),\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{12})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $1.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $\\frac{1}{4}$ and $1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198384 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58fbdce5-60b9-5682-a84e-1c8a5077c762" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Image of a Horizontal Segment Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "430e8d34-03b9-591d-9c8b-3e31f116c812" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276072} {:answer/id #uuid "486f4a48-a6ae-542e-b3a0-6d01f2273f98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276120} {:answer/id #uuid "b61b5d70-d1e6-5e4a-82bd-2355dd672e84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd5de8b1-d4b1-54f7-94a6-cad99f16910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277417} {:answer/id #uuid "f9abb118-15c8-5f39-ac81-9db0da50fa75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd5de8b1-d4b1-54f7-94a6-cad99f16910b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277417} :answer-field/id #uuid "9db7279b-07f0-58ff-81d0-471d2a4854aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b7f9af3-cd62-5b44-9828-72b23454ff87" :question/math-academy-id "q-161032" :question/problem "![](images/16/16d74107dbbf12749766cd9cea7e38d16e28a59efb997bd224aed73c7830324e.png)\n\nConsider the square region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane, given by\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,2\\le u\\le 4,\\,\\,\\frac{10\\pi }{9}\\le v\\le \\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\},\n$$\n\nand the transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v).\n$$\n\nThe image of the top side of $Δ$ under the action of $T$ is a line segment. Which of the following statements are true regarding the image?\n\nI. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nII. The image makes the angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nIII. The endpoints of the image lie on the circles of radii $\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$ centered at $O.$" :question/worked-solution "To map the entire region $Δ$ onto the $xy$-plane, we consider the transformation of each side. However, in this case, we consider only the top side.\n\nFor the top side, we have $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ and $2\\le u\\le 4.$ \n\nSubstituting $v=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ into our transformation equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x,y) & =T(u,v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v) \\\\  & =(u\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,u\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),\\,2\\le u\\le 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a parametric equation of the line segment shown below.\n\n![](images/bf/bfe75ef6b4ea9ddd72be6501f95fd89dd1e80fb2aed691c3c22b158514141cc1.png)\n\n \n\nNote that the line that this segment lies on passes through the origin. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false, while statement II is true. The slope of the line is\n\n$$\nk=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, our line makes an angle of $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- Statement III is false.\n\n- When $u=2,$ we obtain the point \n$(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $2.$\n\n- When $u=4,$ we obtain the point \n$(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})),$\nwhose distance to the origin is $4.$\n\nSo, the endpoints of the line segment lie on the circles of radii $2$ and $4.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389146 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a4f3877-28ec-525d-bc7b-fb086bbcf483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389148} {:answer/id #uuid "c056697a-8b8b-503a-9760-b2880703e4cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/aa/aaa000e6d8798b9f62cc3afc814931378dee78f1527e46befb5cfd51b0a00249.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389149} {:answer/id #uuid "c026f87d-478e-5c1f-bbc5-a89910646e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4d/4d1341dc970cd8a7c52ee7e0988e40f40254ddb8dee77b7dcba2010a126f9fe0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389150} {:answer/id #uuid "030daf68-f271-5761-bbf4-91a4ff37750b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/83d410ca6de599371a47574cf2c6c3fa659f89dca32c6aadd5596276c3ef0c9b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389151} {:answer/id #uuid "cb82fd7c-a45a-557f-b1c5-bab2e976e278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/6f/6fd834262bbe36779afee02caa6df26520fb4d7e3c451806411e7c2a34ba0ab8.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389152}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a4f3877-28ec-525d-bc7b-fb086bbcf483" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389148} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0491ffc-dbaa-5a2b-9802-8000e5fa5ce9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186389147}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc6f6478-43eb-5df8-9592-d3846d859923" :question/math-academy-id "q-161113" :question/problem "![](images/22/22ed424a27d1674bc7f4316b0b644e3dbac4c9f460e23ea2aa6ee90cb74b4c5b.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=1$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f9/f97f1344d6c3a0fd5cfbe06057193e4fe46f9d368cf32cc174db52ba94243744.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "211b4461-dab7-5b44-b44c-46bc27340974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/c8/c8662ff0f722b1c0b651bc35291242713ca57c271dc1011be75d2217e3f95f3c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275777} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce8c0ee-40be-53e8-988d-a7104453c9a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f5cb3c952c7346f6457e082b3f815eca8e6e8f4e4d54ea96448977f2cfc4d7e6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275882} {:answer/id #uuid "44ad264e-d8b9-5718-aa0c-a15d5dca96e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ff/ff3ef277236f696894e85fef3cd7c2cff227f5a1844628d5f34fb613644b43d2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276082} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecb1a60-5044-5467-a26c-127307d4018e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/e6/e6239ef3eb407b37db7029088b6671af710b38f81622735087205bfae0bde67f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276178} {:answer/id #uuid "f50787c0-6f9c-51f6-9b2c-48e520c4c6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277622}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f50787c0-6f9c-51f6-9b2c-48e520c4c6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/83/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277622} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f480fe2-0aa5-5551-a22f-d284129d7b5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc9dfc88-f183-5e04-83ce-439434807dc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161118" :question/problem "![](images/83/8360f06b0d9e9633aa00b779702d386cd403d7fe8ac8a28b51922dca92ddafed.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\sqrt{3},\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=0$ and $u=\\sqrt{3},$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{4},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/83/832658a3d3a6add5f39be27844d2df16a07576ac8578b03b69ebac784b5b4c61.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198375 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b327bc4-3944-515e-8b78-f0dbcf55bc74" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Rectangular Preimage Given Its Image Under a Polar Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49e58033-a329-548c-abff-9edc218c7598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423466} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6bd7f5-9b66-5353-af53-c30d3946e682" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/44/441279c1475c240da098572074b3a2f49e151e5260543770095898a543556c6c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423478} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e92430-5470-58ee-8c3d-b7ebea8e1e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/fe/fe2e21582c6a283d82b37f45a19dfba6334b0d4664c39353fdff8dbcdcaddef6.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423481} {:answer/id #uuid "bb2c862b-d1d4-5b56-a1cd-8af893b2b99a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5c/5ce8905501312d2cf64d2acf0e605e2e66d7fe6d0d28aa29335ab893521062b5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423482} {:answer/id #uuid "f5301a08-27d2-5bf8-a354-e253c4c2c7e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/98/98eb493dc4a73d5fab02d2494285a337623f9502322cf9fe2690f52110d05eea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423488}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49e58033-a329-548c-abff-9edc218c7598" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/5e/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423466} :answer-field/id #uuid "75f4502e-563a-563a-b13c-0f58a709c7ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b6d5f3b-6c7c-587f-b086-0bc40be20551" :question/math-academy-id "q-161159" :question/problem "![](images/38/383b3750be20f665159215eea6b0134c0da930f574e7ef12ff7127b78ac22acb.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ in the $xy$-plane, shown above. The transformation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT:(u,v)\\to (x(u,v),\\,y(u,v)),\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v,\n$$\n\nmaps the region $Δ$ from the $uv$-plane to $D.$ Which of the following could be the region $Δ?$" :question/worked-solution "For our transformation $T,$ we have\n\n$$\nx=u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v,\\,y=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v.\n$$\n\nNotice that if we change $u$ to $r$ and $v$ to $\\theta ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich represents the usual formulas for changing from polar to Cartesian coordinates. \n\nTherefore, the pre-image $Δ$ of $D$ under the action of $T$ is the region that can be written as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 3,\\,\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\le v\\le \\frac{7\\pi }{8}\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is the rectangular region enclosed between\n\n- two vertical lines with Cartesian equations $u=\\frac{1}{2}$ and $u=3,$ and\n\n- two horizontal lines with Cartesian equations $v=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ and $v=\\frac{7\\pi }{8},$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/5e/5eb48411cf0c9efd97bc99a4c5f6edd064664c98eeef87ceda6d9909a5056eab.png)"}]}
